WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.199
Hello, everyone! I'm Mr. Jake. Welcome to Online A two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.499 --> 00:00:09.153
Hi! I'm Sreypich. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.453 --> 00:00:15.344
Unit seven: Customs Around the World. In this unit, you will compare greetings and customs in different countries.

4
00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.142
Custom.

5
00:00:17.842 --> 00:00:20.736
Custom means a usual way of doing something in a place.

6
00:00:21.036 --> 00:00:23.267
Taking off your shoes at home is a custom.

7
00:00:23.967 --> 00:00:26.376
Your turn. Say: Custom.

8
00:00:29.076 --> 00:00:29.972
Custom.

9
00:00:30.983 --> 00:00:31.706
Greeting.

10
00:00:32.406 --> 00:00:35.241
Greeting means something you say or do when you meet someone.

11
00:00:35.541 --> 00:00:37.320
A smile is a friendly greeting.

12
00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:40.452
Your turn. Say: Greeting.

13
00:00:43.152 --> 00:00:43.874
Greeting.

14
00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:45.617
Bow.

15
00:00:46.317 --> 00:00:48.967
Bow means to bend your head or body to show respect.

16
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:51.441
In Japan, people bow to say hello.

17
00:00:52.141 --> 00:00:54.038
Your turn. Say: Bow.

18
00:00:56.738 --> 00:00:57.473
Bow.

19
00:00:58.483 --> 00:00:59.542
Handshake.

20
00:01:00.242 --> 00:01:03.368
Handshake means holding and shaking someone's hand when you meet.

21
00:01:03.668 --> 00:01:06.028
In the USA, people give a handshake.

22
00:01:06.728 --> 00:01:09.473
Your turn. Say: Handshake.

23
00:01:12.173 --> 00:01:13.093
Handshake.

24
00:01:14.083 --> 00:01:15.003
Sampeah.

25
00:01:15.703 --> 00:01:18.736
Sampeah means the Khmer greeting with hands pressed together.

26
00:01:19.036 --> 00:01:21.128
We sampeah our teachers every morning.

27
00:01:21.828 --> 00:01:24.051
Your turn. Say: Sampeah.

28
00:01:26.751 --> 00:01:27.578
Sampeah.

29
00:01:28.583 --> 00:01:29.422
Polite.

30
00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:31.924
Polite means showing good manners.

31
00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:34.618
It is polite to greet older people first.

32
00:01:35.318 --> 00:01:37.785
Your turn. Say: Polite.

33
00:01:40.485 --> 00:01:41.358
Polite.

34
00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:43.177
Culture.

35
00:01:43.877 --> 00:01:47.246
Culture means the ideas, art and customs of a group of people.

36
00:01:47.546 --> 00:01:49.476
I love learning about Khmer culture.

37
00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:53.142
Your turn. Say: Culture.

38
00:01:55.842 --> 00:01:56.646
Culture.

39
00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:58.593
Respect.

40
00:01:59.293 --> 00:02:02.523
Respect means a feeling that someone is important.

41
00:02:02.823 --> 00:02:04.614
We show respect to our elders.

42
00:02:05.314 --> 00:02:08.082
Your turn. Say: Respect.

43
00:02:10.782 --> 00:02:11.760
Respect.

44
00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:14.715
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:15.015 --> 00:02:18.280
Sreypich, how do people greet each other in Cambodia?

46
00:02:19.583 --> 00:02:21.455
In Cambodia, people sampeah.

47
00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:24.041
And in Japan?

48
00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:29.047
In Japan, people bow. In the USA, people shake hands.

49
00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.978
Why are customs important?

50
00:02:33.283 --> 00:02:35.178
They show respect for our culture.

51
00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:40.317
Grammar focus: In plus country, people plus verb.

52
00:02:40.617 --> 00:02:43.653
In Japan, people bow. In Cambodia, people sampeah.

53
00:02:43.953 --> 00:02:47.880
Use the present simple for customs because they happen again and again.

54
00:02:48.883 --> 00:02:51.710
Now read and listen. Hello Around the World.

55
00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:54.102
Every country has its own customs.

56
00:02:55.417 --> 00:02:58.229
In Cambodia, people sampeah to show respect.

57
00:02:59.517 --> 00:03:04.175
In Japan, people bow, and in the USA, people give a handshake.

58
00:03:05.483 --> 00:03:08.690
In Thailand, people wai, which looks like our sampeah.

59
00:03:09.983 --> 00:03:13.248
When we learn about other cultures, we can be polite everywhere.

60
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:16.982
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

61
00:03:17.282 --> 00:03:18.852
In Japan, people blank.

62
00:03:19.152 --> 00:03:22.991
A, bow. B, bows. C, bowing.

63
00:03:26.983 --> 00:03:28.902
The answer is A: bow.

64
00:03:30.417 --> 00:03:33.264
The Khmer greeting with hands pressed together is the blank.

65
00:03:33.564 --> 00:03:37.298
A, sampeah. B, handshake. C, bow.

66
00:03:41.283 --> 00:03:43.097
The answer is A: sampeah.

67
00:03:44.583 --> 00:03:46.374
Showing good manners is blank.

68
00:03:46.674 --> 00:03:50.304
A, polite. B, famous. C, local.

69
00:03:54.317 --> 00:03:56.119
The answer is A: polite.

70
00:03:57.617 --> 00:04:01.476
Now it's your turn to speak. How do people greet each other in your country?

71
00:04:06.776 --> 00:04:12.981
In Cambodia, people sampeah to show respect. In other countries, people bow or shake hands.

72
00:04:13.281 --> 00:04:15.072
Excellent answer, Sreypich!

73
00:04:16.083 --> 00:04:21.360
Great work today! You learned eight new words and in plus country, people plus verb.

74
00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.799
Today's value is respect. Thank the people who help us.

75
00:04:25.099 --> 00:04:27.404
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

76
00:04:27.704 --> 00:04:30.530
Thank you, Mr. Jake! Bye, everyone!

